# Independently authored SHOW empty-result stubs sentinel
# smoke for the GPL MTR-format runner. NOT copied or adapted
# from MySQL's mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (do NOT duplicate):
#   - show_basic_extra.test pins SHOW DATABASES / TABLES /
#     COLUMNS / CREATE TABLE / TABLES LIKE. Its header
#     mentions in prose that VARIABLES / STATUS / PROCESSLIST
#     / GRANTS "all parse and execute without error but
#     return empty result sets" and explicitly says they are
#     "NOT pinned positively in the body". This slice closes
#     that gap.
#
# This slice pins:
#   - SHOW VARIABLES — empty result, no error.
#   - SHOW STATUS — empty result, no error.
#   - SHOW PROCESSLIST — empty result, no error.
#   - SHOW GRANTS — empty result, no error.
#   - SHOW ENGINES — empty result, no error.
#   - SHOW WARNINGS — empty result, no error.
#   - SHOW ERRORS — empty result, no error.
#   - SHOW CHARSET — empty result, no error.
#   - SHOW PRIVILEGES — empty result, no error.
#   - SHOW COLLATION — RETURNS ONE ROW (utf8mb4_general_ci).
#     OUTLIER: the only SHOW in this group that returns data.
#
# Big-picture finding:
#   zeta's SHOW surface accepts 10 commands that MySQL apps
#   commonly probe for capability detection, but 9 of them
#   return empty result sets (no rows). The 10th, SHOW
#   COLLATION, hardcodes a single row representing the
#   default UTF-8 collation. This means MySQL apps doing
#   `SHOW VARIABLES LIKE '%timeout%'` to detect connection-
#   timeout config will get empty results (no error), and
#   apps doing `SHOW COLLATION` to detect charset support
#   get exactly one row.
#
#   The sentinel value: if a future implementation lands any
#   of these (e.g. SHOW VARIABLES starts returning real
#   server vars), the corresponding row will start returning
#   rows and break the test. That's intentional — the test
#   triggers a re-evaluation of whether the new behavior is
#   the right behavior, AND ensures the .result file gets
#   updated to capture the new output.
#
#   Empty-result detection: the MTR runner format emits an
#   echo line for the SQL followed by NOTHING when zero rows
#   are returned (no header, no data). When a row IS
#   returned, the header line + data line both appear. So
#   the format reliably distinguishes "empty" from "non-
#   empty". (Verified: SHOW COLLATION below records the
#   header + data, while the others record only the echo.)
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: SHOW VARIABLES / STATUS / PROCESSLIST /
#     GRANTS / ENGINES / WARNINGS / ERRORS / CHARSET /
#     PRIVILEGES all execute without error but return EMPTY
#     result sets. Apps doing capability probing with these
#     get no rows back.
#   # zeta-adapt: SHOW COLLATION returns ONE row:
#     utf8mb4_general_ci, charset utf8mb4, id 45, default
#     yes, compiled yes, sortlen 1.

--echo === SHOW VARIABLES — empty stub ===

SHOW VARIABLES;

--echo === SHOW STATUS — empty stub ===

SHOW STATUS;

--echo === SHOW PROCESSLIST — empty stub ===

SHOW PROCESSLIST;

--echo === SHOW GRANTS — empty stub ===

SHOW GRANTS;

--echo === SHOW ENGINES — empty stub ===

SHOW ENGINES;

--echo === SHOW WARNINGS — empty stub ===

SHOW WARNINGS;

--echo === SHOW ERRORS — empty stub ===

SHOW ERRORS;

--echo === SHOW CHARSET — empty stub ===

SHOW CHARSET;

--echo === SHOW PRIVILEGES — empty stub ===

SHOW PRIVILEGES;

--echo === SHOW COLLATION — OUTLIER, returns one row ===

SHOW COLLATION;

--echo === SHOW WARNINGS pattern variants — also empty ===

# MySQL allows SHOW WARNINGS LIMIT n, SHOW COUNT(*) WARNINGS,
# etc. Pin the LIMIT variant; the count form may parse
# differently (skip for now to keep slice focused).
SHOW WARNINGS LIMIT 10;
